## Answers to BPMN & DMN Open Questions (with Page Citations)

### BPMN Question 1: Where does "warn at 31 nodes" come from?
- **It does NOT come from *7PMG* (Mendling, Reijers & van der Aalst 2010, `mendling-2010-7pmg`).** In *7PMG*, **Guideline G1** is stated purely qualitatively (*"Use as few elements in the model as possible"*, Section 3.3 p. 10, Table 1 p. 12 / IST p. 131), and **Guideline G7** states a **50-element threshold** (*"Decompose a model with more than 50 elements"*, because the logistic regression error probability exceeds 50% for models with $> 50$ elements, Section 3.3 p. 11, Table 1 p. 12 / IST p. 131).
- **The 31-node threshold comes from Mendling, Sánchez-González, García & La Rosa (2012), *Thresholds for error probability measures of business process models*, Journal of Systems and Software 85(5):1188–1197 (`mendling-2012-thresholds-error-probability`):**
  - **Table 3 & Table 4 (PDF p. 9 / JSS p. 1193):** ROC curve analysis over $n = 2,003$ industrial EPC models identifies the optimal discriminating threshold for `nodes` as **`31.5`** ($\text{AUC} = 0.841, p = 0.015$). Under Bender's Value of an Acceptable Risk Level (VARL), the probability of control-flow errors is **9%** below 31.5 nodes and rises sharply toward 100% above it (**Fig. 3, PDF p. 11 / JSS p. 1195**).
  - **Section 5.1 (PDF p. 11 / JSS p. 1195) & Table 6: Ten Process Modeling Rules (PDF p. 12 / JSS p. 1195):** Explicitly refines 7PMG's G1 and G7:
    - **Rule G1 (`Nodes`):** *"Do not use more than 31."*
    - **Rule G7 (`Nodes`):** *"Decompose a model with more than 31 nodes."*
  - Furthermore, **Avila et al. (2018/2021, `avila-2021-process-modeling-guidelines-slr`, Table 3.2 p. 29)** synthesizes both thresholds alongside Sánchez-González et al. (2013), distinguishing **A1 Total Elements $\le 37$** (comprehension efficiency), **A1/B7 Nodes $\le 31$** (error probability, Mendling et al. 2012) or **$\le 50$** (7PMG G7), and **A2 Activities $\le 31$**.

### BPMN Question 2: What are Leopold, Mendling & Günther (2016)'s model and company counts, and do they use the term "banana model"?
- **Model & Company Counts (`leopold-2016-bpmn-quality-issues-industry`):** Confirmed — Leopold, Mendling & Günther (2016) analyze **585 BPMN 2.0 process models from 6 companies** across **35 modeling guidelines** grouped into **Structure**, **Layout**, and **Labeling** (Section 1 p. 1, Fig. 1 & Section 2 pp. 2–3).
- **Do they use the term "banana model"?** **No.** Neither Leopold, Mendling & Günther (2016) nor any of the other 24 ingested papers uses the phrase *"banana model"*.
  - In **Leopold et al. (2016, Fig. 1 p. 2 & Section 2 pp. 2–3)**, the formal **Layout** defect categories are:
    1. *Inappropriate spacing between elements* ($< 50\%$ of element size; cited in Avila 2018 Table 3.4 C6, p. 40),
    2. *Incorrect modeling direction* (*"arcs flowing into the wrong direction"*, pp. 2–3),
    3. *Inconsistent incoming and outgoing behavior* (p. 2),
    4. *Overlay of edges and nodes* (p. 2), and
    5. *Excessive diagram size* (**47% of models exceed 1 DIN A3 page**, p. 3).
  - The closest colloquial phrase in the literature is **"zigzag models"**, used by **Figl & Strembeck (2015, `figl-strembeck-2015-flow-direction-experiment`, Section 8, p. 12 / GI LNI P-248 p. 70)** to describe diagrams whose control flow bends back and forth to fit a page or avoid scrolling.

### BPMN Question 3: Do Zimoch et al. (BPMDS 2017) show experts and novices failing alike on complex models?
- **Yes, on high-complexity BPMN models, with an important methodological qualification on how "experienced" is defined:**
  - Across both BPMDS 2017 papers (`zimoch-2017-cognitive-insights-preliminary`, Section 4.3 p. 9; `zimoch-2017-eye-tracking-lessons-learned`, Section 3 Lesson C9 p. 10), participants ($n = 36$; $30$ valid eye-tracking datasets) are split at the sample median of prior process-modeling exposure (**`20.5 hours`**) into **Novices / Inexperienced** ($< 20.5\text{ h}$, $n = 15$, $14$ valid eye-tracking) and **Intermediates / Experienced** ($\ge 20.5\text{ h}$, $n = 21$, $16$ valid eye-tracking). As the authors note in Lesson **C9 (Paper 8, p. 10)**, the "experienced" cohort is more accurately described as **intermediates** rather than multi-year domain experts.
  - **Empirical Trajectory Across Easy, Medium, and Hard BPMN Models (`zimoch-2017-cognitive-insights-preliminary`, Table 1 p. 10, Figs. 6–7 p. 11, Table 2 p. 12 / LNBIP 287 pp. 146–148):**
    - **Easy BPMN Model:** Intermediates outperform novices on net comprehension score (**$0.50$ [SD $0.50$] vs. $0.30$ [SD $0.82$]**, on $[-1,+1]$), with fewer fixations (**$98$ vs. $123$**, $p = 0.085$), shorter gaze path (**$21,366\text{ px}$ vs. $25,882\text{ px}$**), and shorter duration (**$29,671\text{ ms}$ vs. $39,305\text{ ms}$**, $p = 0.070$).
    - **Medium BPMN Model:** Comprehension accuracy already converges (**$0.30$ [SD $0.80$] vs. $0.33$ [SD $0.82$]**, $p = 0.842$), though intermediates still process the diagram with fewer fixations (**$154$ vs. $199$**, $p = 0.064$) and significantly faster completion time (**$47,264\text{ ms}$ vs. $63,685\text{ ms}$**, $p = 0.038^*$).
    - **Hard BPMN Model (Both Groups Fail Alike):** Both groups collapse into **negative net comprehension scores** with no statistical difference between experienced and novice subjects:
      - **Intermediates ($\ge 20.5\text{ h}$):** Net score **$-0.32$ (SD $0.85$)**, Fixations **$228$ (SD $71$)**, Gaze path **$40,829\text{ px}$**, Duration **$65,693\text{ ms}$**.
      - **Novices ($< 20.5\text{ h}$):** Net score **$-0.17$ (SD $0.92$)** ($p = 0.352$), Fixations **$231$ (SD $88$)** ($p = 0.906$), Gaze path **$41,904\text{ px}$** ($p = 0.842$), Duration **$75,406\text{ ms}$** ($p = 0.361$).
  - In **Paper 8 (`zimoch-2017-eye-tracking-lessons-learned`, Table 2 p. 8 & Lesson C8 pp. 9–10 / LNBIP 287 pp. 160–162)**, overall BPMN accuracy drops from **$0.66$ (Easy)** and **$0.62$ (Medium)** to **$0.27$ (Hard)** while mental arousal (`LF/HF` ratio) increases from **$1.98 \to 2.53 \to 2.91$**, and Lesson **C8** concludes that with increasing model difficulty, comprehension degrades to the same extent for experienced and inexperienced users alike.

### DMN Question 1 (Paper 19): Are there thresholds such as `"inputs ≤ 5"` or `"NOBE ≤ 25"` in Hasić & Vanthienen (2019)?
- **No — Gemini fabricated those thresholds.** Neither **Paper 18 (`hasic-2019-measuring-complexity-dmn`, BPM 2018 Workshops LNBIP 342)** nor **Paper 19 (`hasic-2019-complexity-metrics-dmn`, *Computer Standards & Interfaces* 65:15–37, 2019, 54-page accepted manuscript)** states any cutoff threshold such as `"inputs ≤ 5"` (`TNIV ≤ 5`) or `"NOBE ≤ 25"`:
  - **What Paper 19 says about `TNIV` (Total Number of Input Variables, Section 4.6, manuscript p. 21):** Stated only qualitatively: *"It is good practice to prevent this number from growing too large. In the running example in Figure 2, 4 input variables are found, i.e. Residency, Comfort, Adults, and Children."* Across the 11 evaluated decision tables (**Table 8, p. 32**), `TNIV` ranges from `1` to `6` and is the second-best decision-table complexity predictor ($r = 0.59, \text{SSD} = 48.00$), behind **Hit Policy Usage (`HPU`, $r = 0.77, \text{SSD} = 29.26$)**, which assigns subjective complexity penalty weights by hit policy (`U = 0.1`, `A = 0.3`, `F = P = 0.4`, `C< = C> = 0.7`, `C+ = C# = 0.8`, Section 4.4 p. 20).
  - **What Paper 19 says about `NOBE` (Number of Basic Elements = `#decisions + #inputs + #informationRequirements`, Section 3.1.3, manuscript p. 9):** Across the 11 evaluated DRD models (**Table 7, p. 31**), `NOBE` ranges from `8` to `43` (`9, 8, 13, 20, 29, 20, 43, 21, 20, 29, 40`) and correlates poorly with perceived complexity in the $n = 113$ survey ($r = 0.01, \text{SSD} = 122.56$). Instead, the authors conclude (Section 6.2 pp. 29–31 & Section 8 p. 34) that the **Durfee Square Metric (`DSM`, $r = 0.35, \text{SSD} = 90.71$)** and **Perfect Square Metric (`PSM`, $r = 0.55, \text{SSD} = 115.72$)** are the most suitable DRD complexity metrics.

### DMN Question 2 (Paper 21): What is the colour result for decision tables vs. decision trees in Djurica, Kummer, Mendling & Figl (2023/2024), with statistical significance?
- In **Djurica, Kummer, Mendling & Figl (2023/2025, *European Journal of Information Systems* 34(1):21–45, `doi:10.1080/0960085X.2023.2270456`, and *EMISA 2024 Extended Abstract*, GI LNI pp. 35–37, `djurica-2024-decision-model-comprehension-emisa`)**, $n = 139$ participants answered 32 comprehension questions in a $2 \times 2 \times 2$ within-subject design crossing **Type** (Table vs. Tree), **Structure** (Expanded vs. Frugal/Contracted), and **Design** (Monochromatic vs. Coloured):
  - **Descriptive Comprehension Accuracy Across All 8 Conditions (EJIS Table 3):**
    - **Decision Trees (Colour IMPROVES accuracy):**
      - Expanded Trees: **Monochromatic `69.67%` (SD `0.46`) $\to$ Coloured `75.82%` (SD `0.43`)** ($+6.15\text{ pp}$).
      - Frugal Trees: **Monochromatic `81.76%` (SD `0.39`) $\to$ Coloured `85.00%` (SD `0.36`)** ($+3.24\text{ pp}$).
      - Overall Decision Trees: **`75.72%` (Monochromatic) $\to$ `82.38%` (Coloured)**.
    - **Decision Tables (Colour DECREASES accuracy):**
      - Expanded Tables: **Monochromatic `78.28%` (SD `0.41`) $\to$ Coloured `76.84%` (SD `0.42`)** ($-1.44\text{ pp}$).
      - Frugal Tables: **Monochromatic `92.83%` (SD `0.26`) $\to$ Coloured `86.47%` (SD `0.34`)** ($-6.36\text{ pp}$).
  - **Binomial Generalized Linear Mixed-Effects Regression & Wald Tests (EJIS Table 4; EMISA 2024 Section 2 p. 36):**
    - **Main Effect of Structure (Expanded vs. Frugal):** **$p < 0.001$** (frugal representations significantly outperform expanded ones).
    - **Main Effect of Type (Table vs. Tree):** **Model 1: $\text{OR} = 0.52, p = 0.035^*$; Model 2: $\text{OR} = 0.51, p = 0.046^*$; Model 3: $\text{OR} = 0.60, p = 0.009^{**}$** (decision tables outperform decision trees).
    - **Main Effect of Design (Monochromatic vs. Colour):** Not significant on its own (**Model 1: $\text{OR} = 0.78, p = 0.423$; Model 2: $\text{OR} = 0.74, p = 0.376$; Model 3: $\text{OR} = 0.87, p = 0.496$**).
    - **Interaction Effect (Type $\times$ Design — Table/Tree $\times$ Monochromatic/Colour):** **Statistically significant in Model 1 ($\text{OR} = 2.12, p = 0.039^*$) and Model 2 ($\text{OR} = 2.20, p = 0.048^*$), and marginal when controlling for task complexity in Model 3 ($\text{OR} = 1.57, p = 0.063$)** — colour highlighting significantly improves comprehension accuracy for decision trees (by guiding visual search along branches) while decreasing comprehension accuracy for decision tables (where eye-tracking indicates dense cell colouring acts as visual distraction, EMISA 2024 p. 36).

### DMN Question 3 (Paper 23): Is there an arXiv copy (`1603.07466`) of Calvanese et al. (2016), and is any human study in it?
- **arXiv Copy (`arxiv-1603.07466v1`):** **Yes** — `https://arxiv.org/pdf/1603.07466.pdf` (16 pages, SHA-256 `8fb29d478d249ef4709fb464821a5b9004e5504d546fb4b120e4475f5140425f`), ingested in full text at `cat-harness/library/arxiv-1603.07466v1/`.
- **Is there any human study in it?** **No.** There is **zero human/user study** in Calvanese et al. (2016). Section 5 (*Implementation and Evaluation*, pp. 14–16) is a **purely computational benchmark** of their geometric sweep-line algorithms for detecting **overlapping rules** (`FindOverlappingRules`, Algorithm 1 p. 11) and **missing rules** (`FindMissingRules`, Algorithm 3 p. 13) implemented in `dmn-js` against the **Signavio DMN editor** across **9 synthetic DMN decision tables** (`499` to `1,506` rules, `3`, `5`, and `7` columns, derived via WEKA J48 trees from a 150,000-row Kaggle credit-lending dataset, p. 14, Tables 2–3 pp. 15–16).

